\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Finite surface gauge data and face weights}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let #{\Sigma} be a connected closed oriented surface of genus
#{g}, with a finite polygonal cell decomposition. Write #{V,E,\mathcal P}
for its vertices, edges and disk faces, and #{P=|\mathcal P|>0}.
Each face has a nonempty cyclic boundary word. Every edge occurs
exactly twice among all face boundaries, with opposite orientations;
the two occurrences may belong to the same face. The link of each
vertex is a circle. These surface conditions are stronger than the
\vocabk{square-cell data}{fdip-006M}: singular quotients are excluded.
Loops and repeated boundary edges are allowed.}

\p{Choose one orientation per edge and a finite group #{G}.
A connection is #{U\in G^E}, with the inverse assigned to a reversed
traversal. Use the \vocabk{ordered-transport convention}{fdip-006N}: if a
face boundary successively traverses #{e_1^{\epsilon_1},\ldots,
e_k^{\epsilon_k}}, its holonomy is
##{U_p=U_{e_k}^{\epsilon_k}\cdots U_{e_1}^{\epsilon_1}.}
Orient all faces by the orientation of #{\Sigma}. Changing a starting
vertex conjugates #{U_p}. A central function, or
[class function](fgap-001M), is a function #{f:G\to\mathbb C}
satisfying #{f(khk^{-1})=f(h)} for all #{h,k\in G}.
It therefore defines the normalized partition sum
##{Z_\Sigma(f)=\frac1{|G|^{|E|}}\sum_{U\in G^E}
 \prod_{p\in\mathcal P}f(U_p).}
This uses uniform probability measure on each edge. Unnormalized
counting gives #{|G|^{|E|}Z_\Sigma(f)}. A complex face weight is allowed
for the algebraic identity; if #{f>0}, division by the partition sum
defines a probability law.}

\p{For a fixed unitary representation #{\sigma} of complex dimension
#{\dim\sigma>0} and #{\beta\ge0}, the
\vocabk{Wilson action}{fdip-006O} corresponds to
##{f_\beta(h)=\exp\left[-\beta\left(
 1-\frac{\operatorname{Re}\chi_\sigma(h)}{\dim\sigma}\right)\right].}
The constant #{e^{-\beta}} per face is part of this weight.
For an oriented closed edge path #{C}, its normalized real Wilson loop is
##{W_\sigma(C)=\frac{\operatorname{Re}\chi_\sigma(U_C)}{\dim\sigma}.}
It is gauge invariant, independent of its starting vertex and unchanged
by reversal. For a general central #{f}, reversal of the chosen surface
orientation need not preserve the weight; the Wilson weight is
inversion invariant.}

\p{Choose one unitary representative of each complex irreducible
representation, indexed by #{\widehat G}, with dimension #{\dim\rho}.
The [character basis](fgap-001M) gives the finite expansion
##{\begin{aligned}
f(h)&=\sum_{\rho\in\widehat G}c_\rho\chi_\rho(h),\\
c_\rho&=\frac1{|G|}\sum_{h\in G}
 f(h)\overline{\chi_\rho(h)}.
\end{aligned}}
These coefficients are not divided by #{\dim\rho} in the expansion.
TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.ClassFunction.apply_eq_sum_characterPairing_mul_character}
formalizes this finite class-function expansion. Its bilinear pairing
is #{|G|^{-1}\sum_h\chi_\rho(h)f(h^{-1})}; substitution by inversion
and unitarity give the displayed coefficient. This correspondence
concerns the expansion, not the surface partition theorem below.}
